This report examines a series of responses associated with multiple offices over two consecutive months in 2016, focusing on four key questions or criteria labeled Q1 to Q4. Each row details an office and their respective answers to these criteria, indicated by "YES" or "NO" at two monthly intervals.

March 2016 presents answers for four distinct offices—Office1 through Office4—highlighting distinct response patterns. Office1 and Office3 tended to provide a mix of affirmative and negative responses, while Office2 returned exclusively negative responses across all questions. Office4 was more positive, indicating "YES" for three out of four questions.

A shift occurs in the subsequent entries corresponding to April 2016, where the data structure varies: office names are absent, and subsequent rows list only results for Q1 through Q4 without explicit office attribution. Despite this, the number of response sets matches the earlier month, suggesting continuity or a similar sample size. There is some repetition in the structure, and another row appears to serve as a header-recap, mapping the order of questions to columns and listing affirmative or negative answers.

The organization and completeness of the records vary, as evidenced by several rows where almost all fields contain placeholder or missing values ("nan"). Additional rows at the end repeat the office names but contain no responses, further suggesting a partial or evolving recording process.

A closer look at the arrangement of answers reveals that "NO" responses dominate in both months, though "YES" responses are more prevalent in certain questions and among specific offices, particularly Office4 in March and in the first April response set. Recognizing these response trends, and considering the incomplete or non-standard entries, provides insight into respondent behavior as well as data collection consistency.

